# agent-hub configuration.
#
# install.sh copies this to /etc/agent-hub.env (mode 0600) if that file does not
# already exist, then you fill it in and `systemctl restart agent-hub`.
#
# On a box that joins a fleet nothing here usually needs changing: the app is
# the control surface, and the sidecar carries what the coordinator sends.
# Every value below is the default unless the comment says otherwise, so a
# line left commented out changes nothing.

# --- Telegram (archived) -----------------------------------------------------
# The Telegram adapter is archived and nothing reads these two lines — see
# docs/telegram.md for why. They are kept so a box configured before that
# keeps its file intact; `agent-hub doctor` says so if either is set.
#AGENT_HUB_TELEGRAM_TOKEN=
#AGENT_HUB_TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USERS=

# --- Web UI ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The HTTP server always runs (the SessionStart hook posts to it). It binds
# loopback by default, which needs no token because reaching it already implies
# shell access on the box.
#
# To publish the UI, keep this on 127.0.0.1 and put a Cloudflare Tunnel in front
# of it — the port then never listens on a routable interface:
#
#   cloudflared tunnel create agent-hub
#   cloudflared tunnel route dns agent-hub hub.example.com
#   # ingress: hostname hub.example.com -> service http://127.0.0.1:8790
#
# Set a token whenever the UI is reachable by anyone but you, tunnel or not.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 24
AGENT_HUB_BIND=127.0.0.1
AGENT_HUB_PORT=8790
AGENT_HUB_TOKEN=

# --- Sessions ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Where sessions start. Must be a directory this user can write; agent-hub marks
# it trusted in ~/.claude.json so sessions never stop at the trust prompt.
#AGENT_HUB_WORKDIR=/root/agent-runs

# Concurrency cap. This counts EVERY live tmux session on the box, not just the
# ones agent-hub started. Raise it only if the box can genuinely take the load —
# the machine this pattern came from hard-froze at around 9 concurrent agents.
#AGENT_HUB_MAX_SESSIONS=5

# Bypass all permission prompts inside sessions (--dangerously-skip-permissions).
# On by default: an unattended session that stops for a prompt is a hung session.
# Override per session without changing this: /new <name> --safe keeps prompts
# on for that one session, /new <name> --dangerous bypasses them. The choice is
# recorded against the session, so resumes and boot restores keep it.
#AGENT_HUB_SKIP_PERMISSIONS=1

# Bring up Remote Control so sessions are drivable from claude.ai/code.
# Set to 0 if your account does not have Remote Control; sessions then have to be
# driven with `tmux attach`.
#AGENT_HUB_REMOTE_CONTROL=1

# Kill a session whose Remote Control never attached, instead of keeping it.
#AGENT_HUB_RC_REQUIRED=0

# Count and show tmux sessions agent-hub did not start. On by default so the cap
# reflects the box's real concurrency. Turn off on a shared box.
#AGENT_HUB_ADOPT_UNTRACKED=1

# --- Resume ------------------------------------------------------------------
# `claude --resume` on a large or stale conversation shows a blocking dialog
# first, asking whether to resume from a summary or in full. agent-hub answers
# it only when it is actually on screen.
#
#   ask      (default) show the dialog — age, token count, both options — and
#            hold the session there until someone chooses. Resuming a 350k-token
#            conversation in full is not a decision worth making blind.
#   summary  always take "Resume from summary (recommended)".
#   full     always resume the full session as-is.
# Naming a mode on the command (/resume <name> full) always wins.
#AGENT_HUB_RESUME_CHOICE=ask

# How long a session may sit waiting for that choice before taking the summary
# option by itself, so a forgotten /resume degrades safely instead of hanging.
#AGENT_HUB_RESUME_ASK_TIMEOUT_MS=600000

# Boot restore can never ask — nobody is present after a 3am reboot — so it
# always uses a concrete choice. summary (default) or full.
#AGENT_HUB_RESUME_CHOICE_UNATTENDED=summary

# Bring back sessions that were running when the box went down.
#AGENT_HUB_RESTORE_ON_START=1

# --- Claude account login ----------------------------------------------------
# Allow `/login` from chat and the web UI so a fresh box can be authenticated
# without SSH. Set to 0 to require SSH for authentication.
#AGENT_HUB_LOGIN=1

# --- Updates -----------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE THIS BOX'S RELEASES COME FROM. The installer fills this in from the
# repository it was installed out of, so a fork's boxes take the fork's
# releases. Unset, a packaged box can report that it cannot update and nothing
# else.
#
# The STABLE address; `releases/latest/download` skips prereleases by GitHub's
# own definition. The rolling address is derived from it, so this is the only
# URL to know.
#AGENT_HUB_RELEASE_MANIFEST=https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/releases/latest/download/manifest.json

# Which releases this box takes: stable (default) or rolling. Setting it HERE
# pins it — the app can then show the answer but not change it, deliberately,
# because a phone quietly writing a value this file overrides would be a screen
# disagreeing with the box. Leave it unset and use `/channel` instead.
#AGENT_HUB_RELEASE_CHANNEL=stable

# --- Misc --------------------------------------------------------------------
#AGENT_HUB_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/agent-hub
#AGENT_HUB_CLAUDE_BIN=claude
#AGENT_HUB_LOG_LEVEL=info
# The deployment itself — what /update pulls into, and what a packaged box
# swaps by moving a symlink. Derived from where this code is running, and worth
# setting only for a layout where the checkout is not the parent of src/.
#AGENT_HUB_INSTALL_DIR=
# Task profiles: one `<name>.md` per profile, whose content becomes a new
# session's first message. A DIRECTORY ON THIS BOX deliberately — the protocol
# carries a profile's name and never its words, so putting one here needs a
# shell here. Missing is normal and means every session starts idle.
#AGENT_HUB_PROFILE_DIR=/var/lib/agent-hub/profiles
# Which unix user the services run as. install.sh writes this and builds the
# sudoers rules around it, so changing it here without `install.sh --repair`
# leaves grants pointing at the old name.
#AGENT_HUB_USER=agent

# --- Sandbox -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Whether a session runs in a podman container at all, and what it runs in.
#
# Off by default, because it needs podman and an image and a box without either
# must keep working. On, the pane becomes `podman run -it`: the session gets
# real root inside a container whose filesystem is discarded on every stop,
# while its conversation and workspace live in volumes that survive.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX=0
# Which podman to run. Only worth setting where the one on PATH is not the one
# that owns these containers.
#AGENT_HUB_PODMAN_BIN=podman
# The image, given IN FULL. A bare name goes through short-name resolution,
# which fails outright on a stock Debian 13; `localhost/` builds here instead
# of pulling. Setting this PINS the image — the app can then show it and not
# change it, the same rule the release channel above follows.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_IMAGE=ghcr.io/thetechnetwork/fleetwright-session:latest
# The short way to say "the same image, mine". A fork sets its own owner and
# gets the same name under it; the full name above still wins.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_IMAGE_OWNER=thetechnetwork
# Resource limits, passed straight to podman. Empty drops the flag entirely.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_MEMORY=8g
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_CPUS=2
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_PIDS_LIMIT=512
# How often a session start may look for a newer image, in milliseconds. Six
# hours: a fix ships within a working day without anybody running /update, and
# the registry is never on the critical path of a start. 0 turns it off.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_REFRESH_MS=21600000
# Build the image on this box the first time it is missing. Off means a box
# with no image refuses to start a session and says so, rather than spending
# ten minutes on it unasked.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_AUTO_BUILD=1
# The Containerfile that build uses. Default: sandbox/Containerfile in the
# install directory.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_CONTAINERFILE=
# The per-session socket the hook and the credential broker speak over, and
# where the sockets live. Off means no hook socket and no credential broker —
# a session can still run, and cannot report or ask for a token.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_HOOK_SOCKET=1
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_HOOK_SOCKET_DIR=/run/agent-fleet
# Extra podman arguments, split like a shell splits a command line: quotes
# group, a backslash escapes. A mount of the root, the container socket, /etc
# or a credential directory is refused — see src/core/sandbox-args.js.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_ARGS=
# TURNS THAT REFUSAL OFF, and the box warns on every start when it is set. It
# exists because a refusal nobody can override gets overridden by deleting the
# check; it is not a defence against whoever can write this file, who owns the
# box already. See docs/hardening.md.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_ALLOW_UNSAFE_ARGS=0

# --- Accounts and credentials -------------------------------------------------
# How often the box renews the Claude credentials it holds, in milliseconds.
# 0 turns it off; the app then says so on the host page.
#AGENT_HUB_CREDENTIAL_KEEPALIVE_MS=3600000
# `agent-hub login` runs the CLI in a tmux session of this name and gives up
# after this long.
#AGENT_HUB_LOGIN_SESSION=agent-hub-login
#AGENT_HUB_LOGIN_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
# How long a forgotten session stays in the recycle bin before its volumes
# are purged. 0 restores the old behaviour, where forget was purge.
#AGENT_HUB_BIN_DAYS=7
# The box's OWN Claude credential file. No longer what sessions run on — see
# docs/one-account-per-person.md — and read for one purpose: it is adopted into
# an account row on first run, so a host that has worked for months goes on
# working under the person it always belonged to.
#AGENT_HUB_SANDBOX_CREDENTIALS=/root/.claude/.credentials.json
# Whose account the surfaces ON THIS BOX use — the web console, and
# `agent-hub new` typed here. Needed only when more than one person has linked
# an account to this machine; with exactly one, that one is the answer.
#AGENT_HUB_OPERATOR=

# --- Timings ------------------------------------------------------------------
# How long to wait for a Remote Control URL to appear after a session starts.
#AGENT_HUB_RC_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# How long to wait for the CLI's resume dialog before deciding it is not
# coming.
#AGENT_HUB_RESUME_DIALOG_WAIT_MS=45000

# --- System ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Whether the built-in web console is served at all. The HTTP server still
# runs either way, because the hook posts to it.
#AGENT_HUB_WEB=1
# Which journalctl and systemctl to run. Only worth setting on a box where the
# ones on PATH are not the ones that manage these units.
#AGENT_HUB_JOURNALCTL_BIN=journalctl
#AGENT_HUB_SYSTEMCTL_BIN=systemctl
# The two answers the installer records so it never asks again: may /upgrade
# run the system package manager, and may /reboot reboot the box. Written by
# install.sh from what you answered, with the matching sudoers rule; changing
# the value here without `install.sh --repair` leaves the rule behind.
#AGENT_HUB_SYSTEM_UPGRADE=0
#AGENT_HUB_SYSTEM_REBOOT=0
